
// IF / ELSE
// Max Score: 2 | Difficulty: Not Specified
// In almost all circumstances, an IF needs to be followed by an ELSE.
// The ELSE block will be executed when the condition in the IF is false.

// Convert the following structured English into Java:

// INPUT a string
// CHECK the type of the 4th character
// IF it is a number, OUTPUT "The 4th character is a number"
// IF it isn't, OUTPUT "The 4th character is not a number"
// The expected input is:
// A string
// e.g. "12fdse45#$%"
// The expected output is:
// "The 4th character is not a number"

// The pseudocode for the IF/ELSE structure is as follows:

// IF <condition> THEN
//   // code
// ELSE
//   // other code
// END IF
// In Java it will look like this:

// if(num > 5){
//   num = 0;
// }else{
//   num++;
// }
DECLARE str:STRING
DECLARE chr:STRING
INPUT str

chr<-SUBSTRING(str,4,1)
IF chr>='0' AND chr<='9' THEN
    OUTPUT "The 4th character is a number"
ELSE 
    OUTPUT "The 4th character is not a number"
ENDIF